<!-- cell: title-page id="06880082-190b-46c7-ba1c-47299af745de" title="The Best Man's Cards" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="a6aef408-2eda-431d-bc93-8e90f26f25a3" -->

Gordon Pike had rehearsed the speech forty-one times, once for every year he had known Dev. He had rehearsed it to the bathroom mirror, to the cat, and, on one low evening, to a man at the bus stop who had not asked to hear it.

The speech lived on six laminated cue cards in the inside pocket of his hired jacket. At four o'clock, when the rain began drumming on the marquee at Hollinsworth Farm, Gordon patted the pocket and found it empty.

He checked the other pocket. He checked his trousers, his shoes, and the cake table, where a steady drip from the canvas roof was hollowing a small crater in the top tier. The cards were gone.

"And now," said the master of ceremonies, "the best man."

Gordon stood. A hundred and twenty faces turned towards him, along with the drip, which had moved to the back of his neck. His mouth opened and nothing came out of it but a small, high noise, like a kettle reconsidering.

In the front row sat Aunt Maud, the groom's great-aunt, who was ninety-two and heard roughly one word in three. She gave him a thumbs-up with tremendous confidence.

"Dev," Gordon began, because Dev's name was the only thing he could remember. "Dev is my oldest friend. When we were nineteen he lent me his last tenner so I could take a girl to the pictures. She left halfway through. He didn't ask for the tenner back." The guests laughed. Gordon felt the floor return beneath his feet.

Then, carried away, he said the one thing he had sworn never to say. "And he failed his driving test seven times."

The marquee went silent. Dev's new mother-in-law put down her glass.

Aunt Maud rose, gripping her stick. "Seven lives!" she announced to the room. "He saved seven lives! I always knew that boy was a hero."

There was a pause, and then the marquee erupted. People stood. Somebody whistled. Dev's mother-in-law dabbed her eyes and raised her glass to her son-in-law, the lifesaver.

Gordon looked at Dev. Dev looked back at him, shrugged very slightly, and mouthed: "Leave it."

So Gordon raised his own glass. "To Dev," he said. "Who has always been there when it mattered."

He sat down to the loudest applause of his life. Later, dancing with Aunt Maud, he found the six laminated cards. She had been using them as a coaster.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="7ad5d4a3-f2a1-4323-ba51-a7fb9cb3a72f" title="Gordon Pike" words="0-15, 250-257, 377-386" -->

The best man and Dev's oldest friend, who has rehearsed his speech forty-one times and loses his cue cards minutes before he must give it.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="f1b2bc4d-165e-4af4-be3d-30ff775aac1b" title="Aunt Maud" words="171-190, 291-296, 398-405" -->

The groom's ninety-two-year-old great-aunt, who hears one word in three. She mishears Gordon's blunder as heroism and turns it into a triumph, and she has had his cue cards all along.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="48734771-1bd1-444f-b83a-10c06080a3d9" title="The marquee at Hollinsworth Farm" words="62-83, 89-115, 316-324" -->

The wedding marquee in a rain-soaked field, its canvas roof dripping onto the cake and down the best man's neck.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="36336f01-d0ab-4e68-ac71-adc758a6456c" title="Stage fright" words="16-45, 132-152, 153-170" -->

Gordon's terror of speaking in front of a crowd. The story never names it; it shows it in the rehearsals, the frozen silence and the noise like a kettle.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="0f260c35-08d5-4fc8-852f-1b2adc3f4af4" title="Friendship" words="217-235, 347-358, 369-376" -->

Gordon and Dev's long friendship: the lent tenner, the secret kept until it slips, and Dev's quiet forgiveness when it does.

<!-- cell: plot id="a3f53fdc-69ea-47ed-8e37-8c7a42fff49e" title="The lost speech" words="0-15, 46-83, 116-119, 120-129, 258-279, 291-315, 316-324, 377-405" -->

Gordon loses his cue cards, improvises, blurts out Dev's secret, and is rescued when Aunt Maud mishears it as heroism; at the end he finds the cards under her glass.
